1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,381
Atelier is an agent built on Gemini 3.5 Flash, called through the Google
GenAI SDK.

2
00:00:06,381 --> 00:00:12,054
Descriptive geometry is a first year subject in architecture and
engineering. Correctness in it is objective.

3
00:00:12,054 --> 00:00:15,953
An isometric axis is at thirty degrees or it is not.

4
00:00:15,953 --> 00:00:20,208
I read eighteen published syllabi and rubrics. Not one states a
tolerance.

5
00:00:20,208 --> 00:00:28,716
The discipline defines correctness exactly, then hands the checking to a
person with a set square at the end of a stack of plates.

6
00:00:28,716 --> 00:00:32,616
Atelier automates that check. It does not invent a new criterion.

7
00:00:35,116 --> 00:00:37,166
Three steps. Drawing, context, critique.

8
00:00:37,166 --> 00:00:44,957
Every sample carries the system it belongs to. Conic, axonometric,
orthographic. Each is measured against a completely different reference.

9
00:00:44,957 --> 00:00:49,468
An isometric cube whose X axis was set six degrees wrong.

10
00:00:51,968 --> 00:00:58,523
X should be 30°. Measured, 36°. Systematic deviation, +6.0°. Y and Z at
zero.

11
00:00:58,523 --> 00:01:09,218
Injected at exactly six degrees, and it comes back at exactly six.
Nothing is guessed. The axes of an isometric projection are 30°, 90° and
150° by definition.

12
00:01:09,218 --> 00:01:13,013
And separating those two numbers says what a published rubric cannot.

13
00:01:13,013 --> 00:01:22,328
A whole family off by the same amount is a set square placed wrong, not
an unsteady hand. One correction before drawing, instead of edge by
edge.

14
00:01:24,828 --> 00:01:27,224
Plane A carries only numbers OpenCV produced.

15
00:01:27,224 --> 00:01:34,070
A validator asks the analysis what it measured and rejects any figure
the model did not get from that set.

16
00:01:34,070 --> 00:01:37,835
Plane B is forbidden a number at all, in either language.

17
00:01:37,835 --> 00:01:45,708
Validated, with the model version underneath. That badge is earned per
critique. When no model answers, the panel turns amber and says so.

18
00:01:48,208 --> 00:01:55,850
A shopping list, uploaded by mistake. Gemini looks at the page before
anything is measured, and declines in its own words.

19
00:01:55,850 --> 00:02:03,857
Without this gate the page goes through RANSAC, finds a vanishing point
among whatever edges exist, and spends real tokens describing nothing.

20
00:02:03,857 --> 00:02:08,224
Measuring the wrong thing carefully is worse than declining to measure
it.

21
00:02:10,724 --> 00:02:16,021
Conic. The reference is inferred. RANSAC estimates the vanishing point
from the student's own lines.

22
00:02:16,021 --> 00:02:23,789
Orthographic, the Monge method. Here the reference is read off the page,
because the ground line is a line the student drew.

23
00:02:23,789 --> 00:02:29,439
This plan sits 18 px sideways. One placement mistake every vertex
inherits, not four broken corners.

24
00:02:29,439 --> 00:02:38,620
The three are not equal, and the first is the weakest. A drifted drawing
produces vanishing points that agree with it, so the reported error
shrinks.

25
00:02:38,620 --> 00:02:46,388
One plate here has 6° of drift injected and scores better than the
perfect one. Axonometry cannot: its axes are constants.

26
00:02:48,888 --> 00:02:56,496
A figure that was not measurable renders as a dash, never a zero. And
the profile is a difficulty level, not a person.

27
00:02:58,996 --> 00:03:03,912
Two Cloud Run services, and these are the requests the run you just
watched made.

28
00:03:03,912 --> 00:03:11,779
A private Cloud Storage inbox with an Eventarc trigger. Cloud Scheduler
for the weekly digest. Firestore, append only, where all of the state
lives.

29
00:03:11,779 --> 00:03:20,956
And Gemini 3.5 Flash on Vertex AI, every call made through the Google
GenAI SDK. In europe-west3, deliberately not the Cloud Run region.

30
00:03:23,456 --> 00:03:26,021
Apache 2.0. One hundred automated tests.

31
00:03:26,021 --> 00:03:35,963
The golden case asserts that a 6° error comes back as 6° to within
0.05°, and the perspective suite cannot hold a bound that tight.

32
00:03:35,963 --> 00:03:38,528
We state the difference instead of hiding it.

